A||||13||olympic||gas @ priceline.com||||1||
Z||000000||olympic||09-11-2000||11:33 PM||||  Anyone here have a priceline.com gas card?  I was reading a thread on another board and some people were saving 40 cents a gallon!!!  It works something like a prepaid phone card.  You bid $X.XX/gallon on a quantity of gas(50 gallons for example)  And the gas companies either accept or reject your price.  If a company accepts you prepay for the 50 gallons of gas and they send you a card that you use to pay at the pump.  Sounds pretty cool.  I'm heading over to <A HREF="http://www.priceline.com" TARGET=_blank>www.priceline.com</A>  right now to see if this is available in Canada.||||reg||1
Z||000001||deceler8||09-13-2000||02:12 PM||||I applied for the Priceline gas card probably six months ago and at that time they indicated it would be sent when the site was up and running.<P>Haven't seen it yet.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fierospeeder||09-13-2000||02:38 PM||||I work at the grocery store, and i was aware of this about a week or two before we started doing the priceline at the registers.<P>I havn't gotten my own card with priceline because i dont really buy groceries.  But the majority of the people who buy groceries this way are saving around 10 dollars depending on how much they buy.<P>My manager did explain to us on how they will be doing this with gas.  This was when chicago's gas prices were 2 bucks a gallon, two months ago??<P>She also told us they get their money from the advertisers on their sites, and the magazine subscriptions people apply for.||||reg||1
Z||000003||LarryB||09-13-2000||02:54 PM||||They had an article about this in the local paper about a week ago. Here's how I understand it works:<P>You start out by selecting up to 5 stations from the list that you're willing to buy the gas from, the amount of gas, and choose from 1 of 4 levels of discount you want that they rate with the odds of being accepted, good, fair, etc.  If you're accepted by one of the stations you get something like a debit card and are set up to buy at the set price from the station that accepted your bid.<P>You need to keep track of the amount of gas you buy, for example if you bought 20 gallons and used 15 for the first fillup, you need to be careful to only put in 5 gallons the next time, else you will be charged the full pump price for the additional gas AND a ($2,50?) service charge.  In that case you would need to put in the 5 gallons using the priceline card, and then reset the pump and fill up using cash or your regular credit card, etc.<P>Supposedly the average savings is about 15 cents per gallon.  Goofing up and getting hit with the service charge pretty much negates the savings. The article left me with the impression that if you're willing to put up with some hassle and keep track of how much gas you buy it's worthwhile but otherwise don't bother. <P>Most of the participating stations are affiliated with national chains, Exxon, Mobil, Shell, etc.  Not so likely to see your local Merit, Irving, Global or similar station. <P>Priceline pays the dealer the full pump price for the gas but how they make up the difference wasn't well explained.  My guess is they use a combination of ad revenue and reselling your name and address to agencies for mass mailing campaigns.<p>[This message has been edited by LarryB (edited 09-13-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Songman||09-13-2000||11:26 PM||||It all sounds great, but I have been seeing little signs pop up all over town that say 'We do now accept Priceline cards'.<P>It is a great thing unless the merchants stop it from being a great thing.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Jonathan||09-14-2000||10:00 AM||||I have received my Priceline gas card.  Had it for about a month now.  I have not wanted to go through all of the trouble of using it.  Maybe with gas prices going back up, I should rethink that!||||reg||1
Z||000006||mrfixit58||09-14-2000||02:49 PM||||I got one and have used it. I wound up saving only a few cents on the gallon. I bought 20 gallons and only used 18.7. The service charge thing (see larry Bs note) kinda scared me off of trying it again. Too much to keep track of right now.<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||Old Lar||09-17-2000||07:52 PM||||I just subscribe to a priceline.com gas card for 50 gallons.  The price I got was 20 cents less that the stations posted price.  I'm waiting to receive the card. I'll let you know how it works out.<P>The station that gave me the price is a Sunoco one that I went to anyway.   The deal is you buy from the correct station and buy the correct grade of gas and only the amount you have prepaid for.  If you screw up its $1.50 per transaction.  There will be some bookeeping to do but at a 20 cents per gallon savings, its does sound encouraging.  I'd use 50 gallons in a month.||||reg||1
Z||000008||LarryB||10-17-2000||03:21 PM||||Just to update, in case any of you missed it, Priceline last week announced that they're discontinuing both the gasoline and grocery services.  <P>Anyone who still has a card with a balance against it is supposed to be sent a refund with a small gift sometime within the next 90 days I believe.  <P>||||reg||11
Z||000009||Old Lar||10-17-2000||08:49 PM||||Yeah I got the card..used it once then the program died.  I got an e-mail that they have credited my account the balance plus what I would have saved if I used the card.  I got one tank of gas 10.125 gallons for $8.00.  Good idea while it lasted.||||reg||1
